.TH X25519TEST 1
.SH NAME
x25519test \- test keyring->curve25519() and the X25519 key-agreement API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B x25519test
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B X25519test
checks
.BR curve25519() ,
.BR curve25519_dh_new() ,
and
.B curve25519_dh_finish()
.RI ( module/keyring.m ,
.IR libinterp/keyring.c ,
.IR libsec/curve25519.c ,
.IR libsec/curve25519_dh.c ,
ported from 9front's own curve25519-donna derived implementation \(em
see
.IR doc/ssl-modernization-plan.md
in the source tree) \(em primitive 3 of the SSL modernization plan,
needed for TLS 1.2's ECDHE-RSA key exchange.
.PP
Four cases check
.B curve25519()
(the raw scalar-multiplication primitive) against vectors generated
from Python's own
.B cryptography
library \(em a real, independent X25519 implementation, not the code
under test, and not hand-computed or recalled from memory: an earlier
attempt to reconstruct RFC 7748's own published vectors from memory
turned out corrupted (an odd number of hex digits), caught before it
was ever used, which is why this test's vectors come from running a
real library instead.
.B Curve25519()
itself does not clamp its scalar argument \(em RFC 7748's own
.B X25519()
function does clamping internally, so the scalars used here are
pre-clamped by hand to match what
.B curve25519_dh_new()
would have done. Checks: a scalar times the standard base point twice
(two different scalars/public keys), and the shared secret computed
from both directions agreeing.
.PP
A fifth case checks
.B curve25519_dh_new()
/
.B curve25519_dh_finish()
\(em the real key-generation API, which uses real randomness and so
can't be checked against a fixed vector \(em as a property instead:
two independently generated key pairs must derive the same non-zero
shared secret from both directions.
.PP
Exits with
.B PASS
if every case held, or
.B FAIL
naming which one and what was expected versus computed.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/x25519test.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR hmacsha256test (1),
.IR tls12prftest (1)
.SH BUGS
Does not exercise the historically tricky low-order-point inputs
directly (RFC 7748 \(sc6.1's own note that certain inputs map to an
all-zero shared secret) other than incidentally: a deliberately
corrupted public key during this test's own development happened to
land on the zero point, reproducing that exact behavior, but no case
here targets it on purpose.
